You are entirely correct, and it is crucial to recognize that the conversation needs to pivot to the profound theological reality of Raj'at (The Return). In Twelver Shia theology, the Raj'at is a fundamental, mandatory belief representing a "specific resurrection" (hashr khass) that takes place on Earth before the actual Day of Judgment. [1, 2, 3]
Rather than a simple historical footnote, Raj'at is the ultimate cosmic climax where absolute good and absolute evil manifest physically. It is the era when the Imams return to rule the world. [1, 2, 3]
1. The Core Meaning: A Separation of Pure Good and Pure Evil
Shia scholars emphasize that Raj'at is not a universal resurrection for all mankind. According to a famous tradition from Imam al-Sadiq, only two specific groups of people will return to Earth during this time: []
The Peak of Faith: The most sincerely righteous believers, including the Prophets, the 12 Imams, and their most loyal, pure followers throughout history. [1, 2, 3]
The Peak of Disbelief: The absolute worst tyrants, oppressors, and enemies of the Divine who historically persecuted the Ahl al-Bayt and the innocent. [1, 2]
2. The Manifestation and the Vanquishing of Evil
The deepest spiritual purpose of Raj'at is earthly justice, retribution, and vindication. While the Day of Judgment is for ultimate heavenly rewards and punishments, Raj'at is designed to humiliate evil where it once thrived—in this physical world. [1, 2, 3]
Exposing the Truth: The tyrants of the past will be resurrected in their physical bodies to face public exposure, accountability, and the destruction of their historical lies. [1]
The Healing of the Oppressed: Believers who were tortured, martyred, or silenced throughout history will be given the joy and honor of witnessing the total defeat of their oppressors. Evil will be completely vanquished and uprooted from the face of the Earth. [1, 2, 4]
3. The Divine Rule of the Imams
You are exactly right regarding the timeline and governance after the Mahdi. The reappearance of Imam al-Mahdi is just the opening act. Following his initial establishment of global justice, the Raj'at of the other Imams begins, initiating a massive epoch of divine governance: [1, 2, 4]
Imam Hussain’s Return: According to major Shia narrations, the very first Imam to return to the world is Imam Hussain. He will return with his companions from Karbala, perform the funeral rites and burial for Imam al-Mahdi, and then assume leadership over the global state. He is prophesied to rule for an incredibly long duration until his old age. [1, 3, 4, 5]
The Kingdom of Muhammad: Following Imam Hussain, Imam Ali (often referred to in eschatological texts as Sahib al-Karrat, the Master of Attacks) and the rest of the Imams, along with the Prophet Muhammad, will return to rule. They will govern the designated lands and realms of the Earth in perfect infallibility. This fulfills the ultimate Quranic promise that "the righteous servants shall inherit the Earth". [1, 2, 3]
4. Why Believing in Raj'at is Crucial
In Twelver Shia Islam, Raj'at is considered a touchstone of true faith. Traditions state that "He is not of us who does not believe in our Raj'at." It is vital because it completes the Shia understanding of divine justice: God's true religion must completely prevail over all others on this physical plane before human history can finally close. [1]
