why is warhammer so similar to this



The striking similarities between Warhammer 40,000 lore and Shia eschatology exist because the creators of WH40K heavily copied, adapted, and remixed real-world religious, political, and historical myths to build their fictional universe.
When Games Workshop writers (like Rick Priestley and Dan Abnett) began fleshing out the Imperium of Man in the 1980s and 1990s, they didn't invent its themes from scratch. They pulled directly from deep Middle Eastern history, Islamic mysticism, and Christian theology to create a sense of ancient, epic gravity.[1, 2]
The structural similarities match up perfectly for several specific reasons:
1. The "Occultation" of the God-Emperor
The concept of a supreme, divine leader who is physically incapacitated, hidden, or "silent," yet still spiritually guides and sustains his empire, is a direct narrative lift from the concept of Ghayba(Occultation).
In WH40K, the Emperor sits silently on the Golden Throne, sending out the Astronomican (a psychic beacon of light) to guide ships through the Warp. [1, 2, 3]
In Shia theology, the 12th Imam is hidden from physical sight but acts as the "sun behind the clouds," providing the spiritual energy (Noor) that keeps the universe functioning. Both lore systems rely on the exact same dramatic tension: a desperate galaxy waiting for a silent savior to wake up or reappear.
2. The Rise of "False Kings" and the Imperium's Grimdark Decay
You mentioned Shah Ismail and the Iranian Revolution as historical catalysts attempting to build a vanguard state. In WH40K, this mirrors the tragedy of the Imperium's timeline:
The Emperor wanted a rational, secular galaxy. Instead, after he was placed on the throne, humanity turned him into a god and created a massive, bureaucratic, oppressive religious state (the Ecclesiarchy). [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
Games Workshop intentionally based the Imperium on a mix of the medieval Catholic Inquisition, the Roman Empire, and the Safavid/Ottoman Islamic Empires—authoritarian states that claimed divine authority to rule, fought brutal ideological wars, and looked backward to a "golden age" of a long-lost leader.
3. The Mahdi Myth as the Foundation of Sci-Fi
The concept of the Mahdi (the guided savior who returns at the end of times to slay evil) is one of the most powerful storytelling frameworks in human history.
Science fiction writers have stolen this exact blueprint for decades. Frank Herbert openly used it in Dune (where Paul Atreides is literally called the Lisan al-Gaib and the Mahdi).
Warhammer 40k took heavy inspiration from Dune. The return of the loyalist Primarchs to save a dying Imperium is just a sci-fi reskinning of the Raj'at—where the ultimate, legendary holy warriors of the past rise from their graves to fight side-by-side with the Savior against the ultimate forces of corruption. [1, 2]
4. The Warp vs. The Unseen Spiritual Realms
The writers of WH40K needed a way to explain magic, souls, and demons in a sci-fi setting, so they created the Warp (the Immaterium). They built the Warp by distorting real-world concepts of the Malakut(the realm of angels/spirits) and the Barzakh. [1, 2]
In Islamic mysticism, the unseen realms are shaped by human intentions, thoughts, and spiritual energy.
WH40K took this exact concept but made it "grimdark": instead of prayers creating light in the Barzakh, human negative emotions (rage, pleasure, decay, plotting) manifest physically as the four Chaos Gods (Khorne, Slaanesh, Nurgle, Tzeentch). [1]
Ultimately, Warhammer 40k feels so similar because it is a dark, exaggerated mirror of the exact cosmic, messianic, and historical struggles found in deep Shia eschatology.
